---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — BSGT"
  description="Why the Bocconi Shipping and Global Trade Student Association exists, what makes it distinct, and what it actually does."
>
  <article class="wrap about">
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>About BSGT</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        BSGT — the Bocconi Shipping and Global Trade Student Association — exists because
        shipping and global trade sit at the centre of nearly everything else: energy, finance,
        manufacturing, geopolitics, sustainability. Bocconi is built around business, economics,
        and management, but until BSGT there was no association bringing students together
        specifically around this subject. BSGT was founded to close that gap.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bocconi already has associations for finance, consulting, and general international
        affairs. BSGT is deliberately narrower and more technical: it exists for students who
        want to go deep on one industry — how ships are financed, chartered, insured, and
        regulated, and how trade actually moves physically rather than just on a balance sheet —
        rather than treat shipping as a footnote inside a broader finance or strategy curriculum.
        That focus is also what makes the association interdisciplinary in practice: understanding
        global trade properly pulls in economics, management, finance, international relations,
        operations, law, and sustainability all at once, and BSGT's members come from all of those
        backgrounds.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do</h2>
      <p>BSGT's work falls into four connected strands.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Industry exposure and guest lectures.</strong> We bring professionals from shipping
        lines, brokers, freight forwarders, ports, and trade finance into direct contact with
        students, so the theory taught in class meets how the industry actually operates.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Knowledge production.</strong> The <a href="/articles">articles</a> BSGT publishes
        are the clearest evidence of this strand — student-written analysis of shipping and trade
        topics, researched and edited to a standard we'd be comfortable putting in front of an
        industry professional.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Practical workshops.</strong> Shorter, skills-focused sessions — chartering,
        documentation, trade finance basics — aimed at giving members working knowledge, not just
        familiarity.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Networking.</strong> Connecting members with each other and with the companies BSGT
        works with, so the association is useful both academically and for the next step after
        Bocconi.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="recruiting">
      <h2>Get involved</h2>
      <p>
        BSGT is looking for students from any programme who are genuinely interested in shipping,
        trade, or maritime economics — you don't need prior experience in the industry, just
        curiosity about it. Membership involves attending events and workshops, and for those who
        want it, contributing to the association's articles or helping run an event.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recruitment runs at the start of each academic term. If you're interested, get in touch —
        we'll point you to whatever the current process is.
      </p>
      <p class="cta">
        <a href="mailto:as.bsgt@studbocconi.it">Get in touch about joining BSGT</a>
      </p>
      <p class="placeholder-note">
        Placeholder contact — replace with the live membership process or form link before launch.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

